  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated severe thunderstorms are possible through\nthe remainder of the afternoon into the evening, mostly across \nnorthern/central portions of the forecast area. Locally heavy \nrainfall is possible which could lead to minor urban flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA Slight Risk of severe thunderstorms across northern/central\nportions of the forecast area this afternoon and evening, with \na Marginal Risk across Downeast areas. Thunderstorms have \ndeveloped in advance of a cold front currently approaching \nwestern portions of the forecast area. A strong mid/upper level\njet is helping provide support for the thunderstorms. \nAdditional support is being provided from a disturbance in \nadvance of an approaching upper trof. The strength of afternoon \nthunderstorms will largely depend on the amount of heating and \nde-stabilization which occurs with greater sun leading to \nincreased instability. CAPE values of 1000-1500 J/kg, with \nDowndraft CAPE values of 1000-1500 J/KG are expected in advance \nof the cold front. Strong to possibly severe thunderstorms will \npersist through the afternoon into the evening in advance of the\ncold front, ending in the wake of the front. Strong/damaging \nwind gusts will be the primary threat with the stronger storms, \nthough large hail could also occur with the more organized \nstorms. In addition, heavy downpours could accompany any \nthunderstorms. This could lead to localized urban/poor-drainage \nflooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dangerous rip currents and high surf along the\nDowneast coast into this evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA High Surf Advisory remains in effect through 8 PM. Unusually rough \nseas by late July standards are expected to peak today. This is \nusually the quietest time of year on the waters, but seas in the \nneighborhood of 6-9 feet are expected up to the coastline, with a 9-\n10 second period swell from the south. People along the coast should \nexercise caution if recreating. Water temperatures in the 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Potential for a system moving in early next\nweek, bringing another round of wetting rainfall.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nA ridge of high pressure will cross the forecast area this \nweekend, and during this time temperatures could build to \nslightly above seasonal averages with highs in the low to mid \n80s. By Monday next week, another shortwave may approach from \nthe west, bringing the next round of moisture into the area that\ncould bring a chance of showers/thunderstorms. A more organized\nsystem is possible Tuesday into Wednesday though details are \nstill uncertain.",
